# Project Canon

## Identity
- Project name: **THRUM** (spelling locked — never Thrust, Trump, Thrumm, etc.)
- Product: Open-source self-hosted “tech heartbeat” monitoring platform (web UI + ingest API + alerts)
- Tagline direction: If it has a pulse, THRUM hears it.
- Repo: GitHub `https://github.com/MohFarzin/Thrum.git` (local folder `Thrum`)
- Owner goal: Freelancer / reusable open-source tool (not a job-placement toy)
- Related future products (mention only in README roadmap; do NOT build now): `Kastora`, `Quicktunnel`

## Source of truth
- Full build brief: `thrum-plan.txt` at repo root — follow it; do not invent alternate product names or v1 scope
- This canon file: hard constraints, workflow, and locked decisions agents must never forget
- When the user says “save this” or a fact is clearly required forever → add it here

## Agent / git workflow (locked)
- Agent is the main developer: user shares ideas, edits, and plans; agent executes
- **NEVER commit. NEVER push.** User commits and pushes from their PC
- After every major fix / batch of fixes, agent must give the user a **commit title** and **description** (ready to paste) — not a git commit
- Project targets GitHub; remotes may exist; agent still does not commit or push

## Runtime (locked)
- Server runs **entirely in Docker Compose**: API, React (prod build), PostgreSQL, Redis, Celery worker, Celery beat, reverse proxy
- Agents/clients run anywhere; outbound HTTPS heartbeats only (no inbound on client)
- Also: server-side HTTP and TCP polling from THRUM host containers
- Success bar: `docker compose up --build` works on a clean machine

## Stack (locked)
- Backend: Python 3.12+, Django, Django REST Framework (prefer DRF)
- Frontend: React (Vite) + TypeScript
- DB: PostgreSQL
- Jobs: Celery + Redis (worker + beat)
- Auth UI: prefer Django session + DRF; document choice if JWT
- Agent auth: per-monitor API keys (shown once; **hashed at rest**)
- Prod proxy: Nginx or Caddy serving built React + proxy `/api` (and admin) to Django
- Optimize for low-resource hosts; few containers; no pointless microservices

## v1 MUST ship
- Auth: register / login / logout
- Monitors CRUD: types `http` | `tcp` | `heartbeat`
- Background HTTP/TCP checks; heartbeat ingest API (Simple tier)
- Status `up` | `down` | `unknown`; history + retention (default 14 days)
- Alerts on state transitions: Email (SMTP) + Discord webhook
- React UI pages per plan; Django admin; Compose + `.env.example`; excellent README; basic tests

## v1 MUST NOT build (roadmap only)
- Medium / High agent tiers, Telegram / WhatsApp, billing / multi-tenant SaaS, public status pages product, Quicktunnel, mobile apps, K8s operator, AI anomaly detection
- Design `AlertSender` interface so more channels can be added later

## Security (non-negotiable)
- Hash API keys at rest; show full key once on create/rotate
- Secrets via env only; never commit `.env`
- Object-level ownership; rate-limit heartbeat + login; production `DEBUG=False`
- HTTP schemes only http/https; SSRF mitigations where practical; no logging of keys/webhooks/SMTP passwords

## Implementation order
Phases **0→6** (seven steps) as in `thrum-plan.txt`:
0 skeleton → 1 auth/CRUD → 2 checkers → 3 heartbeat → 4 alerts → 5 harden → 6 polish/docs/tests
- Do not start the next phase until the current phase **exit checklist** passes
- User verifies each phase with the checklist; agent does not invent extra milestones
- **Current phase:** 0 scaffold complete in repo; user must verify with Docker Compose before Phase 1
- **Auth choice (locked):** Django session + DRF SessionAuthentication (same-origin via proxy)

## Phase exit rule
- After each phase: agent gives commit **title + description**; user commits/pushes
- Benchmark = exit checklist for that phase only (manual + `docker compose` where relevant). No fancy perf suite in v1 unless asked

## Do not change without asking
- Product name spelling: THRUM
- Docker-first runtime model
- Stack choices above
- v1 scope boundaries (must / must-not)
- Agent never commits or pushes
- Phase order 0→6
